OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026", "title": "Critical strip-crossing mass on the honeycomb lattice", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/main.pdf", "family": "237", "family_title": "The three-quarter exponent for honeycomb self-avoiding walk", "abstract_html": "At the critical weight of the regular honeycomb lattice, the total weight of self-avoiding paths crossing a strip of height <i>N</i> is comparable to <i>N</i><sup>−1/4</sup>. The first horizontal-displacement moment of return paths is comparable to <i>N</i><sup>3/4</sup>.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1 (Critical strip mass)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The honeycomb lattice is dual to the equilateral triangular tiling of side length one, with one triangular edge direction horizontal. Consecutive horizontal band boundaries have separation \\(d_0=\\sqrt3/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A port is the midpoint of an edge on the boundary of a union of triangles. A path between distinct ports follows dual edges, including its two terminal half-edges, visits each dual vertex at most once, and meets the boundary only at its endpoints.</p><p class=\"li\">The length \\(|\\gamma|\\) is the number of visited dual vertices. Ports carry no weight. The critical path weight is \\(\\rho^{|\\gamma|}\\), where\n  \\[\n  \\rho=\\frac1{\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt2}},\n  \\qquad c=\\cos(3\\pi/8).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S_N\\) be the infinite horizontal strip of \\(N\\) triangular bands, \\(N\\ge1\\). Its bottom ports are indexed consecutively by \\(\\mathbb Z\\), with source at port \\(0\\); adjacent bottom ports have horizontal separation one. Top ports lie on \\(N/2+\\mathbb Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(Z_D(a,b)\\) for the sum of critical weights of paths from \\(a\\) to \\(b\\) in \\(D\\). A bottom-to-bottom path is an arch, and a bottom-to-top path is a bridge. Define\n  \\[\n  K_N(k)=Z_{S_N}(0,k),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal A_N=\\sum_{k\\ne0}K_N(k),\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal B_N=\\sum_{b\\text{ on the top}}Z_{S_N}(0,b),\n  \\qquad\n  m_N=\\sum_{k\\ge1}kK_N(k).\n  \\]\n  All sums run over actual admissible boundary ports.</p><p class=\"li\">The notation \\(f_N\\asymp g_N\\) means that their ratio lies between two fixed positive constants.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(N\\ge1\\), all the displayed sums are finite. With constants independent of \\(N\\),\n\\[\nc\\mathcal A_N+\\mathcal B_N=1,\n\\qquad\nm_{N+1}-m_N\\asymp\\mathcal B_N,\n\\qquad\nm_N\\asymp N^{3/4},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal B_N\\asymp N^{-1/4}.\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(\\mathcal B_N\\) is nonincreasing in \\(N\\).</p><p>If a height-zero term is useful, set \\(\\mathcal B_0=1\\) by convention; no strip path is added. Then\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_N\\asymp(1+N)^{-1/4},\n\\qquad N\\ge0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 1 gives finite-height transfer decay and absolute convergence of the vacuum and source sums. The source pairing of Proposition 3 gives finiteness of \\(m_N\\); the boundary argument of Proposition 4 gives finiteness of the arch and bridge masses and\n\\[\nc\\mathcal A_N+\\mathcal B_N=1.\n\\]\nProposition 4 also gives monotonicity of \\(\\mathcal B_N\\) and\n\\[\nm_{N+1}-m_N\\asymp\\mathcal B_N.\n\\]\nProposition 8 proves \\(m_N\\asymp N^{3/4}\\).</p><p>For the bridge upper bound, sum the lower increment bound over \\(1\\le j&lt;N\\). By monotonicity,\n\\[\n(N-1)\\mathcal B_N\n\\lesssim m_N-m_1\n\\le m_N.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathcal B_N\\lesssim N^{-1/4}\\), absorbing bounded \\(N\\) into the constant.</p><p>For the lower bound, choose a fixed integer \\(L\\) so large that the lower bound for \\(m_{LN}\\) exceeds twice the upper bound for \\(m_N\\). Then\n\\[\nN^{3/4}\n\\lesssim m_{LN}-m_N\n\\lesssim\\sum_{j=N}^{LN-1}\\mathcal B_j\n\\le(L-1)N\\mathcal B_N.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\mathcal B_N\\gtrsim N^{-1/4}\\).</p><p>All constants are independent of \\(N\\). With the convention \\(\\mathcal B_0=1\\), the final estimate is equivalently\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_N\\asymp(1+N)^{-1/4},\\qquad N\\ge0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Positive flux, finite strip transfers, and their exchange and reduction identities.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Positive flux, finite strip transfers, and their exchange and reduction identities.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the path and port conventions above, and put\n  \\[\n  t=\\frac38,\\qquad \\lambda=\\frac\\pi8,\\qquad\n  d=\\cos(2\\lambda)=\\sin(2\\lambda),\\qquad C=\\cos\\lambda,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  c=\\cos(3\\lambda),\\qquad\n  \\rho=C-c=\\frac1{2C}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a spectral parameter \\(u\\), define\n  \\[\n  a(u)=\\cos(2u-3\\lambda),\n  \\qquad\n  L(u)=\\sin(2\\lambda+u)\\sin(3\\lambda+u)=\\frac{C+a(u)}2,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  A(u)=\\frac{d\\sin(3\\lambda-u)}{L(u)},\n  \\qquad\n  U(u)=\\frac{d\\sin u}{L(u)},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  B(u)=\\frac{\\sin u\\sin(3\\lambda-u)}{L(u)}\n       =\\frac{a(u)-c}{a(u)+C},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  E(u)=\\frac{\\sin(3\\lambda-u)\\sin(2\\lambda-u)}{L(u)},\n  \\qquad\n  F(u)=\\frac{\\sin(u-\\lambda)\\sin u}{L(u)}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A geometric column consists of \\(N\\) rhombi stacked along horizontal edges. In row \\(i\\), the other edge makes angle \\(u_i/t\\) with the horizontal. The empty tile has weight one; a single opposite-port connection has weight \\(B\\); bottom–left and top–right turns have weight \\(A\\); the other turns have weight \\(U\\); the two-turn configurations have weights \\(E\\) and \\(F\\), respectively. Port occupancies must match under gluing, and every closed component has weight zero.</p><p class=\"li\">The planar diagram algebra has ordered vacant or occupied input and output slots. Composition glues matching occupancies and annihilates a mismatch or closed loop; tensor product is juxtaposition; transpose reflects a diagram.</p><p class=\"li\">The two-slot operator \\(R(v)\\) has coefficients \\(1,A(v),B(v),U(v),E(v),F(v)\\) on the empty diagram, a single vertical connection, a single diagonal connection, a cap alone or cup alone, two vertical connections, and a cap together with a cup, respectively. There are two choices for a single vertical or diagonal connection.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal L_N^0\\) have basis the noncrossing partial pairings of occupied cut slots, with other slots vacant. Let \\(\\mathcal L_N^1\\) have the same pairings and one additional unpaired occupied slot connected to an exterior source. This defect cannot be enclosed by a pair.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(X=(u_1,\\ldots,u_N)\\), set\n  \\[\n  M_X=R_N(v_N)\\cdots R_1(v_1),\n  \\qquad v_i=3\\lambda-u_i.\n  \\]\n  Its ordered inputs are \\((\\mathrm{aux},1,\\ldots,N)\\), and its ordered outputs are \\((1,\\ldots,N,\\mathrm{aux})\\). Locally the ordered inputs are \\((\\mathrm{south},\\mathrm{west})\\), and outputs are \\((\\mathrm{east},\\mathrm{north})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">With both auxiliary ends vacant, \\(M_X\\) defines\n  \\[\n  T_X^{(\\epsilon)}:\\mathcal L_N^\\epsilon\\longrightarrow\\mathcal L_N^\\epsilon,\n  \\qquad \\epsilon=0,1;\n  \\qquad T_X=T_X^{(0)}.\n  \\]\n  With south occupied and north vacant it defines\n  \\(b_X:\\mathcal L_N^0\\to\\mathcal L_N^1\\). Interchanging these conditions defines \\(b_X^{\\mathrm{north}}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Superscript \\(^{-}\\) denotes the corresponding right-to-left sweep. Horizontal reflection replaces \\(X\\) by\n  \\[\n  X^*=(3\\lambda-u_1,\\ldots,3\\lambda-u_N),\n  \\qquad\n  M_X^-=R_N(u_N)\\cdots R_1(u_1).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The splitter \\(S_2\\), from one slot to two, sends a vacancy to a vacancy plus \\(\\rho\\) times a cup, and an occupied strand to either output slot with coefficient \\(\\rho\\). The splitter \\(S_3\\), from no slots to two, is the vacant diagram plus a cup.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The boundary cancellation identity and the local operator identities in Lemmas 1.1–1.2 hold. At every list\n\\[\nu_i\\in\\{0,\\lambda,2\\lambda,3\\lambda\\},\n\\]\nthe restricted vacuum transfer and the one-source transfer have spectral radii strictly less than one, also for reversed columns and in suitable complex neighborhoods with absolute tile weights controlling convergence.</p><p>There is a unique normalized stationary vacuum \\(P_N(X)\\), with empty coordinate one. Its entries are rational in \\(e^{iu_1},\\ldots,e^{iu_N}\\), and regular at all the specified lists.</p><p>If \\(X'\\) exchanges rows \\(i,i+1\\) and \\(\\delta=u_{i+1}-u_i\\), then\n\\[\nM_X(I_{\\mathrm{aux}}\\otimes R_i(\\delta))\n=(R_i(\\delta)\\otimes I_{\\mathrm{aux}})M_{X'}.\n\\]\nIf \\(u_{i+1}=u_i+s\\lambda\\), \\(s=2,3\\), let \\(X_{\\mathrm{red}}\\) replace these rows by \\(u_i+\\lambda\\) when \\(s=2\\), or delete both when \\(s=3\\). Then\n\\[\nM_X(I_{\\mathrm{aux}}\\otimes S_s)\n=(S_s\\otimes I_{\\mathrm{aux}})M_{X_{\\mathrm{red}}},\n\\]\n\\[\n(S_s^{\\mathsf t}\\otimes I_{\\mathrm{aux}})M_X^-\n=M_{X_{\\mathrm{red}}}^-(I_{\\mathrm{aux}}\\otimes S_s^{\\mathsf t}).\n\\]\nThese identities also hold after imposing vacant-end or either source-insertion conditions. In particular,\n\\[\nT_X^{(\\epsilon)}R_i(\\delta)=R_i(\\delta)T_{X'}^{(\\epsilon)},\n\\qquad\nb_XR_i(\\delta)=R_i(\\delta)b_{X'},\n\\]\n\\[\nT_X^{(\\epsilon)}S_s=S_sT_{X_{\\mathrm{red}}}^{(\\epsilon)},\n\\qquad\nb_XS_s=S_sb_{X_{\\mathrm{red}}},\n\\]\n\\[\nS_s^{\\mathsf t}T_X^{(\\epsilon),-}\n=T_{X_{\\mathrm{red}}}^{(\\epsilon),-}S_s^{\\mathsf t},\n\\qquad\nS_s^{\\mathsf t}b_X^-=b_{X_{\\mathrm{red}}}^-S_s^{\\mathsf t},\n\\]\nand likewise with \\(b^{\\mathrm{north}}\\) in place of \\(b\\). Consequently,\n\\[\nP_N(\\ldots,u_i,u_{i+1},\\ldots)\n=R_i(u_{i+1}-u_i)P_N(\\ldots,u_{i+1},u_i,\\ldots),\n\\]\n\\[\nP_N=S_sP_{\\mathrm{red}}\n\\qquad\\text{when }u_{i+1}=u_i+s\\lambda.\n\\]</p><p>At \\(u_1=3\\lambda\\), the first slot is vacant and can be deleted. At \\(u_1=0\\), the first-slot-vacant part is \\(P_{N-1}\\); the occupied part is obtained from \\(P_{N-1}\\) by one column on rows \\(2,\\ldots,N\\), with a south source and vacant north, tying the source to slot \\(1\\).</p><p>Finally, if \\(D_k\\) changes the sign of states with slot \\(k\\) occupied, then\n\\[\nP_N(X+\\pi e_k)=D_kP_N(X).\n\\]\nAll exchange and reduction statements are identities of rational functions, specialized where regular or removable.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 supplies positive boundary control, Lemma 1.2 supplies the local identities, and Lemma 1.3 supplies convergence and uniqueness.</p><p>The geometric and operator weights agree because\n\\[\nA(3\\lambda-u)=U(u),\\qquad\nU(3\\lambda-u)=A(u),\\qquad\nB(3\\lambda-u)=B(u),\n\\]\n\\[\nE(3\\lambda-u)=F(u),\\qquad\nF(3\\lambda-u)=E(u).\n\\]\nAt \\(u=\\lambda\\) or \\(2\\lambda\\), splitting along the short diagonal recovers two triangular tiles, with weight \\(\\rho\\) per visited triangle. At \\(u=0\\) or \\(3\\lambda\\), the row is flat and its routes continue deterministically through adjacent left or right ports, with two disjoint routes allowed with weight one.</p><p>Apply the braid relation to adjacent rows to obtain the exchange identity for \\(M_X\\). The tensor positions account for the different input and output orders. For a reduction \\(u_{i+1}=u_i+s\\lambda\\), the splitting identity has argument \\(x=v_{i+1}\\). In the merge case,\n\\[\nx+\\lambda=3\\lambda-(u_i+\\lambda),\n\\]\nso the effective row is the stated one. Transpose splitting and reverse the slot order to obtain the reflected projection identity.</p><p>All three column identities leave auxiliary endpoints untouched. Imposing the vacant or source conditions gives the corresponding transfer and insertion identities. In a source-insertion identity, the cut map on the right acts in the vacuum sector and the cut map on the left in the one-source sector.</p><p>For the stationary vacuum identities, the column equations show that each proposed right-hand side is stationary. Its empty coordinate is one: a cap producing an empty output from a nonempty pairing would close a zero-weight loop. Uniqueness from Lemma 1.3 proves exchange near the all-zero list, and reduction near a mixed list with \\(u_i=0\\). Intersecting these regular neighborhoods with the specialization hypersurface gives an open set in that hypersurface. Rational continuation proves the identities generally. Reflected projection and source identities are used in the same way, with uniqueness of the corresponding stationary or inhomogeneous transfer equation.</p><p>The first-slot formulas follow from\n\\[\nR(0)=I,\\qquad R(3\\lambda)=S_3S_3^{\\mathsf t}.\n\\]\nFor the latter identity, an occupied first input with vacant auxiliary input contributes zero.</p><p>Finally, shifting \\(u_k\\) by \\(\\pi\\) multiplies each tile weight by the occupancy signs at its west and east ports. It conjugates \\(T_N\\) by \\(D_k\\). Since \\(D_k\\) preserves the empty coordinate, uniqueness gives\n\\[\nP_N(X+\\pi e_k)=D_kP_N(X).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1 (Boundary identity)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\) be a finite simply connected union of triangles with simple polygonal boundary, and let \\(a\\) be a boundary port.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the critical weight \\(\\rho^{|\\gamma|}\\) and \\(t=3/8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W(\\gamma)\\) be the total signed turning of \\(\\gamma\\), measured from its inward initial direction to its outward final direction.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sum_{b\\in\\partial D}\\ \\sum_{\\gamma:a\\to b}\n\\rho^{|\\gamma|}e^{itW(\\gamma)}=1.\n\\]\nIf \\(D\\) is convex, its unsigned boundary partition sum is at most \\(1/c\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Send mass one along the inward half-edge at \\(a\\). At each newly visited vertex split it between the two possible continuations, multiplying by \\(\\rho e^{i\\lambda}\\) and \\(\\rho e^{-i\\lambda}\\), respectively. The identity \\(2\\rho\\cos\\lambda=1\\) conserves total mass. Stop a branch when it exits \\(D\\) or is about to enter a vertex already visited. This produces a finite tree because \\(D\\) has finitely many vertices.</p><p>For a stopped collision, the newly closed cycle is simple and has a disjoint incoming stem. The stem lies outside the cycle: it starts on \\(\\partial D\\) and cannot cross the cycle before their first common vertex. Consequently the unused branch at that vertex is exterior to the cycle. The cycle turn there is \\(\\pi/3\\) in the counterclockwise orientation, rather than \\(-\\pi/3\\); the clockwise statement is reversed. The turn from the stem into the cycle replaces this closing turn by its negative. The two ways to traverse the cycle therefore have winding increments \\(4\\pi/3\\) and \\(-4\\pi/3\\) relative to the stem. They have the same unsigned weight and cancel, since\n\\[\ne^{it(4\\pi/3)}+e^{-it(4\\pi/3)}=0.\n\\]\nCycle reversal is an involution on all stopped collisions. The remaining terminal mass gives the identity.</p><p>For a path ending at \\(b\\), close it using the counterclockwise boundary arc from \\(b\\) to \\(a\\). The turns at the two ports are \\(\\pi/2\\), so the turning theorem for this simple closed curve gives\n\\[\nW(\\gamma)=\\pi-\\operatorname{turn}_{\\partial D}(b\\to a).\n\\]\nFor a convex boundary the latter turn lies in \\([0,2\\pi]\\). Thus \\(|W(\\gamma)|\\le\\pi\\) and\n\\[\n\\cos(tW(\\gamma))\\ge\\cos(3\\pi/8)=c.\n\\]\nTaking real parts proves the unsigned bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.2 (Local diagram identities), with its proof from Appendix A", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(R,S_2,S_3,\\lambda\\) and the zero-loop diagram algebra of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(R_i\\) for action in slots \\(i,i+1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\widehat R_2(x)=R(x+\\lambda),\n  \\]\n  while \\(\\widehat R_3(x)\\) is the one-slot identity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nR_2(y)R_1(x+y)R_2(x)\n=R_1(x)R_2(x+y)R_1(y),\n\\]\n\\[\nR(s\\lambda)=S_sS_s^{\\mathsf t},\n\\qquad s=2,3,\n\\]\n\\[\nR_2(x)R_1(x+s\\lambda)(I\\otimes S_s)\n=(S_s\\otimes I)\\widehat R_s(x),\n\\qquad s=2,3,\n\\]\n\\[\nR(x)R(-x)=I.\n\\]\nThese are rational-function identities. They may be specialized wherever the relevant sides are regular, including at removable singularities; an individual operator at a genuine pole is not assigned a finite value.</p>", "proof": "<p>The factorization \\(R(s\\lambda)=S_sS_s^{\\mathsf t}\\) follows by direct substitution.</p><p>For the splitting identity, let subscripts \\(1,2\\) mean arguments \\(x+s\\lambda,x\\). Let \\(q\\) be the cup coefficient and \\(k\\) the strand coefficient of the splitter:\n\\[\nq=k=\\rho\\quad(s=2),\n\\qquad\nq=1,\\ k=0\\quad(s=3).\n\\]\nUnsubscripted weights refer to \\(\\widehat R_s\\); in the deletion case use \\(B=1\\) and set its other nonempty two-slot coefficients to zero. The inputs are the passing auxiliary strand and the slot to be split; the latter is absent in the deletion case. Outputs \\(1,2\\) are the split slots and output \\(3\\) is the auxiliary slot. Input bits record occupancy. For one input strand, a bare output index specifies its destination and a parenthesized pair specifies an additional cup.</p><p>The complete coefficient check is\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n\\text{input}&amp;\\text{connections at output}&amp;\n\\text{left side}&amp;(S_s\\otimes I)\\widehat R_s\\\\ \\hline\n00&amp;\\varnothing&amp;1&amp;1\\\\\n&amp;12\\text{ (cup)}&amp;U_1A_2+qB_1B_2&amp;q\\\\\n&amp;13&amp;U_1B_2+qB_1A_2&amp;kU\\\\\n&amp;23&amp;U_2+qA_1E_2&amp;kU\\\\ \\hline\n10&amp;1&amp;A_1+qF_1U_2&amp;kA\\\\\n&amp;2&amp;B_1A_2+qU_1B_2&amp;kA\\\\\n&amp;3&amp;B_1B_2+qU_1A_2&amp;B\\\\\n&amp;3,(12)&amp;qF_1E_2&amp;qB\\\\\n&amp;1,(23)&amp;A_1U_2+q(E_1E_2+F_1F_2)&amp;0\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nFor \\(s=2\\), there are also\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n01&amp;1&amp;k(B_1+U_1U_2)&amp;kB\\\\\n&amp;2&amp;k(A_1A_2+B_2)&amp;kB\\\\\n&amp;3&amp;k(A_1B_2+A_2)&amp;A\\\\\n&amp;1,(23)&amp;k(B_1U_2+U_1F_2)&amp;0\\\\\n&amp;3,(12)&amp;kU_1E_2&amp;qA\\\\ \\hline\n11&amp;\\varnothing&amp;k(U_1+B_1U_2)&amp;U\\\\\n&amp;12&amp;k(E_1A_2+A_1B_2)&amp;0\\\\\n&amp;(12)&amp;kF_1A_2&amp;qU\\\\\n&amp;13&amp;k(E_1B_2+A_1A_2)&amp;kE\\\\\n&amp;(13)&amp;kF_1B_2&amp;kF\\\\\n&amp;23&amp;kB_1E_2&amp;kE\\\\\n&amp;(23)&amp;k(U_1U_2+B_1F_2)&amp;kF\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nIn the \\(11\\) block, an empty output or a parenthesized output pair connects the two input strands to one another; a bare pair of output indices denotes two through-strands.</p><p>Put \\(s_j=\\sin(x+j\\lambda)\\). The weights at \\(x+j\\lambda\\) have common denominator \\(s_{j+2}s_{j+3}\\) and numerators\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccccc}\n\\text{weight}&amp;A&amp;B&amp;U&amp;E&amp;F\\\\ \\hline\n\\text{numerator}&amp;ds_{j+5}&amp;s_js_{j+5}&amp;ds_j&amp;\ns_{j+5}s_{j+6}&amp;-s_{j+7}s_j\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nSubstitution uses\n\\[\ns_{j+8}=-s_j,\\qquad\ns_js_{j+1}+s_{j+4}s_{j+5}=C,\n\\]\n\\[\ns_2s_6=d^2-s_0^2,\\qquad s_1s_5=d^2-s_7^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho(s_1-s_7)=s_0,\\qquad\n\\rho(s_6-s_0)=s_7,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho(s_1s_2-s_6s_7)=s_0s_4,\\qquad\n\\rho(s_5s_6-s_0s_1)=s_7s_3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho(s_7s_0+s_4s_5)=s_6s_2,\\qquad\n\\rho(s_2s_3+s_7s_0)=s_1s_5,\n\\]\n\\[\ns_1s_6-s_7s_0=\\rho d^2,\n\\]\n\\[\ns_1s_6s_5=s_7s_2s_3+d^2s_0,\\qquad\ns_1s_6s_2=s_0s_4s_5+d^2s_7.\n\\]\nThe last two identities also follow from the preceding formulas for \\(s_1s_5\\) and \\(s_6s_2\\). These substitutions verify every table entry.</p><p>For unitarity, the one-strand sector reduces to multiplying two matrices with diagonal entries \\(A\\) and off-diagonal entries \\(B\\). In the even sector the required identities are\n\\[\nE(x)E(-x)=1,\\qquad U(x)+E(x)U(-x)=0,\n\\]\n\\[\nE(x)F(-x)+F(x)E(-x)+U(x)U(-x)=0.\n\\]\nA cap composed with a cup creates a loop and therefore vanishes. These identities follow from the weight formulas.</p><p>For the braid relation, first set \\(y=s\\lambda\\), \\(s=2,3\\). Use factorization and splitting, together with their transposes and reversal of slot order. The effective box is symmetric, and both sides become\n\\[\n(I\\otimes S_s)\\widehat R_s(x)(S_s^{\\mathsf t}\\otimes I).\n\\]\nUnitarity transports these identities to \\(x+y=s\\lambda\\), by applying the identity with arguments \\((-x,x+y)\\). The case \\(y=0\\) follows from \\(R(0)=I\\).</p><p>Multiply the difference of the braid-relation sides by their three scalar denominators, obtaining a diagram sum \\(\\mathcal F(x,y)\\). Every coefficient is Laurent polynomial in \\(e^{iy}\\), with exponents between \\(-4\\) and \\(4\\). If \\(D_j\\) multiplies a diagram by \\(-1\\) when slot \\(j\\) is occupied, inspection of the nine boxes gives\n\\[\nR_i(v+\\pi)=D_{i+1}R_i(v)D_i.\n\\]\nOn either side of the braid relation the two internal \\(D_2\\) signs cancel. The remaining signs are at input slot \\(1\\) and output slot \\(3\\); the scalar denominators are \\(\\pi\\)-periodic. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal F(x,y+\\pi)=D_3\\mathcal F(x,y)D_1.\n\\]\nEvery boundary coefficient has fixed parity. After multiplication by a Laurent monomial it is a polynomial of degree at most four in \\(e^{2iy}\\). It vanishes at\n\\[\ny=0,\\quad 2\\lambda,\\quad 3\\lambda,\\quad\n2\\lambda-x,\\quad 3\\lambda-x.\n\\]\nThese are distinct modulo \\(\\pi\\) for generic \\(x\\). Interpolation proves the identity, and rational continuation removes the genericity restriction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.3 (Decay and rational continuation)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the transfers of Proposition 1 at a fixed height \\(N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(u_i\\in\\{0,\\lambda,2\\lambda,3\\lambda\\}\\) for every row.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The restriction of \\(T_N\\) to vectors with zero empty coordinate, and \\(T_N^{(1)}\\), have spectral radii strictly less than one. The same holds for reversed columns and in a complex neighborhood of each such parameter list, with absolute tile weights controlling absolute convergence.</p><p>Consequently there is a unique stationary vacuum \\(P_N\\) with empty coordinate one. Its entries are rational in the \\(e^{iu_i}\\) and regular at every specified list.</p>", "proof": "<p>First take a homogeneous triangular parallelogram of height \\(H&gt;0\\) and width \\(M\\). The total weight of paths from a fixed left port to its right side tends to zero as \\(M\\to\\infty\\). To see this, append a column immediately to the right of the exit and continue, entirely inside this new column, to its bottom port. The continuation has at most a fixed number of vertices depending only on \\(H\\), so its weight is bounded below by a positive constant depending only on \\(H\\).</p><p>For every \\(M&lt;L\\), these extensions are paths in a parallelogram of width \\(L\\). Their bottom endpoints identify \\(M\\), and their first exit into the appended column recovers the original path. They are therefore distinct. Writing \\(q_H(M)\\) for the crossing mass and \\(C_H\\) for the maximum number of added vertices, Lemma 1.1 gives\n\\[\n\\rho^{C_H}\\sum_{M&lt;L}q_H(M)\\le1/c.\n\\]\nThus \\(q_H(M)\\to0\\). The same lemma uniformly bounds paths joining ports on the same side.</p><p>For a mixed list of the four allowed parameters, collapse the flat rows. The row vectors belong to\n\\[\n\\{1,e^{i\\pi/3},e^{2i\\pi/3},-1\\}.\n\\]\nOutside two end pieces of horizontal size \\(O(N)\\), the remaining triangles form a homogeneous parallelogram of height \\(H\\), the number of nonflat rows, and width \\(M-O(N)\\). This does not depend on which neighboring triangles were grouped into rhombi. A collapsed deterministic route drifts by at most \\(N\\) columns. The end pieces have bounded size for fixed \\(N\\) and contribute bounded weights.</p><p>Every nonzero diagram contributing to either restricted transfer power has a component connecting the left and right cuts. In the vacuum sector, otherwise the nonempty input pairing would close into a loop; in the source sector, the source must reach the output. Cut all components at the two ends of the homogeneous core. There are only boundedly many endpoint patterns, depending on \\(N\\), and at least one segment spans the core. Ignoring avoidance between these segments bounds their total weight by a product of single-path partition sums. Each nonspanning factor is at most \\(1/c\\).</p><p>For \\(H&gt;0\\), choose core width \\(M-\\kappa_X\\), with \\(0\\le\\kappa_X=O(N)\\). If \\(Q_N\\) is the nonempty vacuum block and \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_{\\max}\\) is the largest absolute matrix entry, then\n\\[\n\\|Q_N^M\\|_{\\max}+\\|(T_N^{(1)})^M\\|_{\\max}\n\\le C_N\\sum_{a\\in\\mathcal E_H}q_{H,a}(M-\\kappa_X)\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\mathcal E_H\\) is the finite set of left ports of the core, \\(q_{H,a}\\) is its crossing mass from \\(a\\), and \\(C_N\\) absorbs the bounded end pieces and endpoint patterns. All constants are for fixed \\(N\\); no uniform spectral gap is needed. If \\(H=0\\), no spanning component is possible once \\(M\\) is sufficiently large. Both restricted matrix powers therefore tend to zero. Since the matrices are finite, their spectral radii are less than one.</p><p>At the specified parameters all tile weights are nonnegative. The matrices obtained by taking absolute tile weights vary continuously, so their restricted spectral radii remain less than one in a small complex neighborhood. This proves absolute convergence. The reflected argument is identical.</p><p>In the empty/nonempty decomposition,\n\\[\nT_N=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n1&amp;0\\\\ v&amp;Q\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nIts normalized stationary vector is\n\\[\n(1,(I-Q)^{-1}v),\n\\]\nwhich is regular in the stated neighborhoods and rational in the spectral variables. This proves uniqueness and continuation. Equivalently, \\(P_N\\) is the sum of finite path diagrams in the vacuum half-strip.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Polynomial normalization of the stationary vacuum.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Polynomial normalization of the stationary vacuum.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use\n  \\[\n  \\lambda=\\pi/8,\\quad d=\\cos(2\\lambda),\\quad\n  C=\\cos\\lambda,\\quad c=\\cos(3\\lambda),\\quad \\rho=C-c,\n  \\]\n  and the normalized vacuum \\(P_N(X)\\) of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(X=(u_1,\\ldots,u_N)\\), put \\(a_i=a(u_i)=\\cos(2u_i-3\\lambda)\\), and define\n  \\[\n  D(a,b)=a^2+b^2+2dab-d^2,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  p_N(a_1,\\ldots,a_N)=\n  \\prod_{i&lt;j}\\frac{D(a_i,a_j)}{a_i-a_j}\n  \\operatorname{Pf}\\!\\left[\n  \\frac{(a_i-a_j)(a_i+a_j+\\rho)}{D(a_i,a_j)}\n  \\right].\n  \\]\n  For odd \\(N\\), augment the skew matrix by a last column of ones and its negative transpose. Set \\(p_0=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(W_N=p_NP_N\\) and \\(W_0=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The scalar \\(p_N\\) is symmetric polynomial of degree at most \\(N-1\\) in each argument, with the leading-coefficient and flat, deletion, and merge identities of Lemma 2.1.</p><p>The vector \\(W_N\\) is Laurent polynomial in \\(e^{iu_1},\\ldots,e^{iu_N}\\), with each exponent between \\(-(2N-2)\\) and \\(2N-2\\). In the last variable \\(y=u_N\\), \\(b=a(y)\\):</p><p class=\"li\">the last-slot-vacant part is polynomial in \\(b\\), of degree at most \\(N-1\\), with leading coefficient \\(W_{N-1}\\);</p><p class=\"li\">the last-slot-occupied part is \\(\\sin y\\) times a polynomial in \\(b\\), of degree at most \\(N-2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 establishes the scalar polynomial and its reduction identities. Lemma 2.2 constructs the polynomial vector, proves its degree bounds and stationarity, and identifies it with \\(p_NP_N\\) using Proposition 1. Together they give the stated polynomial normalization.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1 (Scalar reduction identities)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p_N,D,\\lambda,C,c,\\rho\\) be as in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">In formulas with an argument list \\(Y\\), the subscript of \\(p\\) is the number of arguments.</p><p class=\"li\">For a parameter \\(w\\), put \\(f_j=\\cos(w+j\\lambda)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The function \\(p_N\\) is symmetric polynomial of degree at most \\(N-1\\) in each argument. Its leading coefficient in any argument is \\(p_{N-1}\\) of the remaining arguments, and\n\\[\np_N(Y,c)=p_{N-1}(Y)\\prod_{z\\in Y}(z+C).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\np(Y,f_0,f_6)\n=p(Y)(f_0+f_6+\\rho)\n\\prod_{z\\in Y}(z-f_{-6})(z-f_{12}),\n\\]\n\\[\np(Y,f_{-2},f_2)\n=p(Y,f_0)(f_{-2}+f_2+\\rho)\n\\prod_{z\\in Y}(z+f_0).\n\\]\nBoth formulas also hold with all shifts negated.</p>", "proof": "<p>After multiplication by all \\(D\\) factors, the Pfaffian is an alternating polynomial, hence divisible by the Vandermonde product in the definition of \\(p_N\\). Counting degrees before and after division gives degree at most \\(N-1\\) in each variable.</p><p>Letting a last argument tend to infinity proves the leading-coefficient statement: its kernel entries tend to \\(-1\\). In odd size, first add the augmenting column and row to that argument’s column and row.</p><p>At the flat value,\n\\[\nD(z,c)=(z-c)(z+C),\n\\]\nand the kernel entry equals one. This proves the flat identity.</p><p>At \\(D(f_0,f_6)=0\\), only Pfaffian terms pairing these arguments survive after denominators are cleared. The identity\n\\[\nD(f_0,z)=(z-f_{-6})(z-f_6)\n\\]\nthen gives deletion.</p><p>Prove merge by induction on \\(r=|Y|\\). The case \\(r=0\\) follows from\n\\[\np_2(a,b)=a+b+\\rho.\n\\]\nFor \\(r\\ge1\\), distinguish a spectator \\(z\\). Both sides have degree at most \\(r+1\\) in \\(z\\), and their leading coefficients agree by induction. They vanish at \\(z=-f_0\\): delete this argument with \\(f_{-2}\\), and the remaining factor involving \\(f_2\\) vanishes. At \\(z=c\\), they agree by the flat formula and\n\\[\n(C+f_{-2})(C+f_2)=(c+f_0)(C+f_0).\n\\]\nThey also agree at each deletion value of \\(z\\) with another spectator. Write that pair as \\(g_0,g_6\\), where \\(g_j=\\cos(v+j\\lambda)\\), and use\n\\[\n\\prod_{\\ell=\\pm2}(f_\\ell-g_{-6})(f_\\ell-g_{12})\n=(f_0+g_0)(f_0+g_6)(f_0-g_{-6})(f_0-g_{12}).\n\\]\nThis is the product-to-sum identity obtained from shifts by \\(\\pm2\\lambda\\).</p><p>There are \\(2(r-1)\\) deletion values, in addition to \\(-f_0\\) and \\(c\\). For generic arguments they are distinct and give at least \\(r+1\\) roots for a difference of degree at most \\(r\\), after its leading coefficient has canceled. Polynomial continuation concludes the proof.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2 (Vacuum degree bound)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(P_N,p_N,W_N\\) from Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Deleting a vacant last slot identifies that sector with \\(\\mathcal L_{N-1}^0\\). Deleting an occupied last slot leaves its partner as the exposed defect of a state in \\(\\mathcal L_{N-1}^1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Subscripts \\(v,o\\) denote these last-slot-vacant and last-slot-occupied sectors.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The vector \\(W_N=p_NP_N\\) has the Laurent bounds and last-variable polynomial forms stated in Proposition 2.</p>", "proof": "<p>The assertion for \\(N=1\\) is immediate. Construct the asserted polynomial by induction and then prove stationarity. Throughout the construction, \\(W\\) denotes the candidate at size \\(N\\), whereas each smaller \\(W_j=p_jP_j\\) is already known.</p><p>**Construction and regularity.** For each \\(i&lt;N\\), introduce\n\\[\ny_i=u_i+3\\lambda,\\qquad\n\\beta_i=a(y_i),\\qquad\n\\gamma_i=a(u_i-3\\lambda),\\qquad\n\\sigma_i=a(u_i+6\\lambda).\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathcal R_i\\) move the inserted row \\(y_i\\) from immediately above row \\(i\\) to the last position, using successive boxes with arguments \\(y_i-u_j\\), \\(i&lt;j&lt;N\\). Exchange, reduction, and scalar deletion prescribe at \\(y=y_i\\) the value\n\\[\nY_i=(a_i+\\beta_i+\\rho)\n\\prod_{\\substack{j&lt;N\\\\j\\ne i}}(a_j-\\gamma_i)(a_j-\\sigma_i)\\,\n\\mathcal R_iS_3\nW_{N-2}(X\\setminus\\{u_i,u_N\\}).\n\\]\nThese data agree with the genuine rational vector \\(p_NP_N\\) on the node hypersurface. Check near \\(u_i=0\\), with the other rows mixed, where the stationary vectors are regular. The mixed point supplies an open neighborhood within the hypersurface, so this is a rational identity in the remaining parameters.</p><p>For distinct nodes, put\n\\[\n\\ell_i(b)=\n\\prod_{\\substack{j&lt;N\\\\j\\ne i}}\n\\frac{b-\\beta_j}{\\beta_i-\\beta_j}.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nW_v(b)=W_{N-1}\\prod_{i&lt;N}(b-\\beta_i)\n+\\sum_{i&lt;N}(Y_i)_v\\ell_i(b),\n\\]\n\\[\nW_o(y)=\\sin y\\sum_{i&lt;N}\\frac{(Y_i)_o}{\\sin y_i}\\ell_i(b).\n\\]\nThese have the required last-variable degree forms and node values. Scalar interpolation shows that the empty coordinate is \\(p_N\\).</p><p>Each moving-box denominator divides the factors\n\\((a_j-\\gamma_i)(a_j-\\sigma_i)\\). The possible divisor \\(\\sin y_i\\) also cancels. Explicitly, for \\(x=u_i,v=u_j\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{(a(v)-a(x-3\\lambda))(a(v)-a(x+6\\lambda))}\n{L(x+3\\lambda-v)}\n=-4\\sin(x+v-6\\lambda)\\sin(x+v+3\\lambda),\n\\]\n\\[\na(x)+a(x+3\\lambda)+\\rho\n=4c\\sin(x+3\\lambda)\\sin(3\\lambda-x).\n\\]</p><p>The remaining possible divisors come from colliding nodes. Since\n\\[\n\\beta_i-\\beta_j\n=-2\\sin(u_i+u_j+3\\lambda)\\sin(u_i-u_j),\n\\]\nthe two components are\n\\[\nu_i=u_j\\pmod\\pi,\n\\qquad\nu_i+u_j=-3\\lambda\\pmod\\pi.\n\\]\nOn the first component the data, including divided occupied data, agree by their identities with the true vector and its occupancy signs. This can be checked near the regular mixture\n\\(u_i=u_j=0,\\ y_i=y_j=3\\lambda\\).\nOn the second component both data vanish through their factors involving \\(\\sigma_i,\\sigma_j\\); at a generic point no moving-box denominator vanishes.</p><p>These checks at generic points of every irreducible divisor suffice, since all possible denominators are products of the indicated simple Laurent factors. Hence \\(W\\) is Laurent polynomial in the earlier variables; intersections introduce no further denominators.</p><p>For the degree, send an earlier \\(u_k\\) to either Laurent extreme. The moving boxes remain bounded by the weight formulas. Data with \\(i\\ne k\\) grow at most like \\(a_k^{N-1}\\): they contain two scalar factors and \\(W_{N-2}\\), while \\(\\ell_i(b)\\) stays bounded. Data with \\(i=k\\) grow at most like\n\\[\na_k^{\\,1+2(N-2)},\n\\]\nwhile \\(\\ell_k(b)=O(a_k^{-(N-2)})\\). The leading-coefficient term has the same bound. Since poles are removed, the growth bounds every earlier exponent between \\(-(2N-2)\\) and \\(2N-2\\).</p><p>Interpolation preserves the occupancy sign under \\(u_k\\mapsto u_k+\\pi\\); when the shift affects a node, its last-slot sign cancels the sign of the dividing sine.</p><p>**Relations in the earlier rows.** Earlier-row braid covariance follows by last-variable interpolation. The exchange box is independent of the last variable, leading coefficients agree by induction, and every node datum agrees by the rational vacuum exchange identity. Uniqueness of the interpolant gives covariance.</p><p>Two additional specializations provide the zeros needed for stationarity.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If adjacent earlier rows satisfy \\(u_{r+1}=u_r+s\\lambda\\), \\(s=2,3\\), the interpolant equals the smaller split vector multiplied by the scalar quotient from merge or deletion in Lemma 2.1. The quotient has degree one in \\(b\\) for merge and two for deletion, with monic last-variable factor. Required forms and leading coefficients agree by induction.</p><p>   At a node indexed by \\(i\\ne r+1\\), compare both expressions with the true vector near a regular simultaneous specialization: take \\(u_r=0\\), \\(u_i=0\\) when distinct, \\(y_i=3\\lambda\\), and the remaining rows mixed. At \\(i=r+1\\), both expressions vanish. On the split side the vanishing factor is \\(b-\\sigma_r\\) for deletion or \\(b+a(u_r+\\lambda)\\) for merge. On the node-data side it is \\(a_r-\\gamma_i\\) or \\(a_r-\\sigma_i\\), respectively; no moving box crosses row \\(r\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. At \\(u_1=0\\) or \\(3\\lambda\\), use the first-slot formulas of Proposition 1, replacing the smaller vacuum by \\(W_{N-1}\\) and multiplying by\n   \\[\n   \\prod_{j=2}^N(a_j+C).\n   \\]\n   For the occupied first-slot part, the south-source column’s last box, with north vacant, contributes \\(1\\) or \\(A(y)\\) to a vacant last output, and \\(U(y)\\) or \\(B(y)\\) to an occupied last output. Multiplication by \\(b+C\\) clears the denominator and gives the required forms. Only the contribution \\(1\\) has the leading vacant degree, leaving the last-row-omitted formula.</p><p>   At nodes the expressions agree at regular mixed specializations, taking \\(u_i=0\\) for \\(i&gt;1\\). The exception \\(u_1=3\\lambda,i=1,y_i=6\\lambda\\) is handled by the vanishing factors \\(a(y_i)+C\\) and \\(a_1+\\beta_1+\\rho\\).</p><p>On each specialization the nodes are generically distinct and their dividing sines nonzero; continuation handles all other cases.</p><p>**Stationarity.** Consider\n\\[\nV=\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^NL(u_j)\\right)(T_N-I)W.\n\\]\nIt vanishes at each last-variable node, since those data came from the genuine stationary vector.</p><p>Put \\(n=N-1\\),\n\\[\nq=W_o/\\sin y,\\qquad \\Lambda_n=\\prod_{j&lt;N}L(u_j).\n\\]\nBesides \\(b_n^{\\mathrm{north}}\\), use\n\\(C_n^{\\mathrm{north}}:\\mathcal L_n^1\\to\\mathcal L_n^0\\), obtained from a column with north occupied and south vacant by tying its north end to the exterior defect of the input. The output is a vacuum pairing; a diagram closing a loop contributes zero. These maps and the first-\\(n\\)-row transfers are independent of \\(y\\).</p><p>Using\n\\[\nL(y)=\\frac{b+C}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\sin y\\sin(3\\lambda-y)=\\frac{b-c}{2},\n\\]\nthe exact last-box blocks are\n\\[\nV_v=\\frac{\\Lambda_n}{2}\n\\left((b+C)(T_n-I)W_v\n+d(b-c)C_n^{\\mathrm{north}}q\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{V_o}{\\sin y}=\\frac{\\Lambda_n}{2}\n\\left((b-c)T_n^{(1)}q-(b+C)q\n+2db_n^{\\mathrm{north}}W_v\\right).\n\\]\nThe second has degree at most \\(N-1\\). In the first, the only possible degree-\\(N\\) coefficient is\n\\[\n[b^N]V_v=\\frac{\\Lambda_n}{2}(T_n-I)W_n=0\n\\]\nby inductive stationarity. Thus \\(V_v\\) also has degree at most \\(N-1\\), and every coordinate of \\(V\\) contains\n\\[\n\\prod_{i&lt;N}(b-\\beta_i).\n\\]\nIts Laurent exponents in each earlier variable are bounded by \\(2N\\).</p><p>The two specializations above make \\(V\\) vanish when an earlier row is \\(0\\) or \\(3\\lambda\\), or when\n\\[\nu_j=u_i+s\\lambda,\\qquad i&lt;j&lt;N,\\quad s=2,3.\n\\]\nMove arbitrary pairs next to one another without interchanging them, using earlier-row covariance and the column identity; generic invertibility follows from unitarity.</p><p>For fixed \\(k&lt;N\\), these zeros force divisibility by\n\\[\n(b-\\beta_k)\\sin u_k\\sin(u_k-3\\lambda)\n\\prod_{\\substack{j&lt;k\\\\s\\in\\{2,3\\}}}\n\\sin(u_k-u_j-s\\lambda)\n\\prod_{\\substack{k&lt;j&lt;N\\\\s\\in\\{2,3\\}}}\n\\sin(u_j-u_k-s\\lambda).\n\\]\nFor generic remaining variables this divisor has Laurent extremes \\(\\pm2N\\): \\(b-\\beta_k\\) contributes two in each direction, the two flat sine factors contribute two, and the pair factors contribute \\(2N-4\\). Its parity under \\(u_k\\mapsto u_k+\\pi\\) is even.</p><p>The quotient of a Laurent polynomial with the same exponent bound by this divisor is independent of \\(e^{iu_k}\\). If slot \\(k\\) is occupied, the coordinate of \\(V\\) has odd parity, so it is zero. Every nonempty vacuum pairing has an occupied earlier slot, since it has at least two occupied slots. The empty residual is identically zero. Hence \\(V=0\\), so \\(W\\) is stationary. Its empty coordinate is \\(p_N\\); uniqueness at generic regular parameters gives \\(W=p_NP_N\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 4.1 (Arch formula)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the rhombic weights, transfers, and stationary vacuum of Proposition 1, and \\(p_N\\) from Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">For a repeated column \\(X=(u_1,\\ldots,u_N)\\) near a physical parameter list, let \\(K_X(k)\\) be the bottom-to-bottom partition sum with displacement \\(k\\), exactly two occupied exterior endpoints, and one connected path. Define\n  \\[\n  m(X)=\\sum_{k\\ge1}kK_X(k).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  H_N=(I-T_N^{(1)})^{-1}b_NP_N,\n  \\]\n  and \\(H_N^-\\) in the reflected strip.</p><p class=\"li\">The bilinear pairing \\(\\operatorname{Glue}\\) of one-source states is one when occupancies agree and gluing joins the two sources into one component without loops, and zero otherwise.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  a_i=\\cos(2u_i-3\\lambda),\\qquad\n  \\eta=2d-1,\n  \\qquad\n  g_X(z)=\\frac{p_{N+1}(a_1,\\ldots,a_N,z)}\n                    {p_N(a_1,\\ldots,a_N)}.\n  \\]\n  For \\(N=0\\), use \\(m(\\varnothing)=0\\), \\(g_0=1\\), and \\([z^{-1}]g_0=0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The source sums converge absolutely near every mixed physical list, and\n\\[\nm(X)=\\operatorname{Glue}(H_N^-,H_N).\n\\]\nThe function \\(m\\) is rational in the spectral variables and regular at those lists. For generic \\(X\\), \\(g_X\\) is monic of degree \\(N\\), and\n\\[\nm(X)=\\sum_{i=1}^Na_i-\\eta[z^{N-1}]g_X(z).\n\\]\nThe right-hand side is understood by continuation at removable singularities.</p><p>At \\(X=(\\lambda,\\ldots,\\lambda)\\), the rhombi split into the triangular bands of the honeycomb strip, so \\(a_i=C\\), \\(m(X)=m_N\\), and\n\\[\nm_N=NC-\\eta\\lim_{a_1,\\ldots,a_N\\to C}[z^{N-1}]g_X(z).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Monicity of \\(g_X\\) follows from the leading-coefficient identity in Proposition 2.</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\widetilde m(X)=\\sum_i a_i-\\eta[z^{N-1}]g_X(z).\n\\]\nThe scalar identities in Proposition 2 give\n\\[\ng_{\\mathrm{merge}}(z)=(z+f_0)g_{\\mathrm{red}}(z),\n\\]\n\\[\ng_{\\mathrm{delete}}(z)\n=(z-f_{-6})(z-f_{12})g_{\\mathrm{red}}(z),\n\\]\n\\[\ng_{\\mathrm{flat}}(z)=(z+C)g_{\\mathrm{red}}(z).\n\\]\nUsing\n\\[\nf_{-2}+f_2-f_0=\\eta f_0,\n\\qquad\nf_0+f_6=-\\eta(f_{-6}+f_{12}),\n\\qquad c=\\eta C,\n\\]\none sees that \\(\\widetilde m\\) has the same three reductions as \\(m\\).</p><p>By Lemma 3.2, both \\(p_N^2m\\) and \\(p_N^2\\widetilde m\\) have degree at most \\(2N\\) in every \\(a_i\\). Induct on \\(N\\). In one distinguished variable, every other argument \\(a_j=\\cos\\theta_j\\) supplies two merge values\n\\[\n\\cos(\\theta_j\\pm4\\lambda)\n\\]\nand two deletion values\n\\[\n\\cos(\\theta_j\\pm6\\lambda),\n\\]\nin addition to the flat value \\(c\\). These give \\(4N-3\\) distinct roots for generic remaining arguments, more than \\(2N\\) for \\(N\\ge2\\). The polynomial difference vanishes.</p><p>The initial cases are \\(m(\\varnothing)=0\\) and the single-row computation of Lemma 3.1. This proves the formula.</p><p>At \\(X=(\\lambda,\\ldots,\\lambda)\\), each rhombic row splits into the two triangular tiles of one band, with weight \\(\\rho\\) per visited vertex. Thus \\(a_i=C\\) and \\(m(X)=m_N\\). Regularity from Proposition 1 gives the removable coefficient limit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "pairing, symmetries, and row reductions used in Proposition 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(m(X),H_N,H_N^-,g_X\\), and \\(\\operatorname{Glue}\\) from Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">At an ascending merge or deletion specialization, let \\(S_s\\) and \\(X_{\\mathrm{red}}\\) be as in Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The pairing formula for \\(m\\) holds and defines a rational function regular near the mixed physical lists. This function is symmetric in the rows, invariant under \\(u_j\\mapsto u_j+\\pi\\) and under the separate reflections\n\\[\nu_j\\longleftrightarrow3\\lambda-u_j,\n\\]\nand reduces to the smaller-strip moment under merge, deletion, or omission of a flat row \\(a_j=c\\).</p><p>At one row,\n\\[\nm(u)=\\frac{A(u)U(u)}{(1-B(u))^2}\n=\\frac{a(u)-c}{1+d}\n=a(u)-\\eta(a(u)+\\rho).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A cut can meet the same arch several times. Either half then contains its source fragment together with paired fragments. The vacuum \\(P_N\\) supplies paired fragments before the source column, while the resolvent in \\(H_N\\) sums columns between the source and the cut.</p><p>Expanding the two resolvents places one source on either side of the cut. An arch of endpoint separation \\(k\\) has exactly \\(k\\) cuts between its endpoints. Hence\n\\[\nm(X)=\\operatorname{Glue}(H_N^-,H_N).\n\\]\nProposition 1 justifies absolute convergence of the source and vacuum sums near every mixed parameter list. It also gives rationality and regularity.</p><p>Moving an exchange box through both halves of the pairing, using the transfer and source equations of Proposition 1, proves symmetry. At an ascending merge or deletion specialization,\n\\[\nP_N=S_sP_{\\mathrm{red}},\n\\qquad H_N=S_sH_{\\mathrm{red}}.\n\\]\nThe reflected projection gives\n\\[\nS_s^{\\mathsf t}P_N^-=P_{\\mathrm{red}}^-,\n\\qquad\nS_s^{\\mathsf t}H_N^-=H_{\\mathrm{red}}^-.\n\\]\nFor the first equality, the projected vector is stationary with empty coordinate one. For the second, its source equation is\n\\[\n(I-T_{\\mathrm{red}}^{(1),-})S_s^{\\mathsf t}H_N^-\n=b_{\\mathrm{red}}^-S_s^{\\mathsf t}P_N^-\n=b_{\\mathrm{red}}^-P_{\\mathrm{red}}^-.\n\\]\nUniqueness near a mixed specialization and rational continuation identify the projected source vector. Transposing the splitter inside the pairing reduces \\(m\\) to the smaller strip. The two halves reduce by insertion and projection, respectively; no identity \\(S_s^{\\mathsf t}S_s=I\\) is required. Occupancy signs cancel under a \\(\\pi\\) shift.</p><p>For separate reflection symmetry, use exchange to put the relevant row on top. Since all top ports are vacant, each visit to that row is an interval that turns up and back down. An interval with displacement \\(\\ell\\ge1\\) has weight\n\\[\nAU B^{\\ell-1},\n\\]\ninvariant under \\(u_j\\leftrightarrow3\\lambda-u_j\\). The intervals are ordered and disjoint. This holds for absolutely convergent sums near physical parameters and hence rationally. If \\(a_j=c\\), then \\(AU=0\\), these excursions vanish, and the flat row can be omitted.</p><p>The single-row sum is the stated geometric-series expression; substituting the weight formulas gives its two equivalent forms.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Source-sector polynomial bound used in Proposition 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Regard \\(a_i=\\cos(2u_i-3\\lambda)\\) as the polynomial variables.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Both \\(p_NH_N\\) and \\(p_NH_N^-\\) are Laurent polynomial in the \\(e^{iu_i}\\), with each exponent bounded in absolute value by \\(2N\\). Consequently \\(p_N^2m(X)\\) is a symmetric polynomial in \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_N\\), of degree at most \\(2N\\) in each argument.</p>", "proof": "<p>First use a north source. Add a last row \\(y\\) to the vacuum strip, and identify its occupied last-slot sector with one-source states on the earlier rows. Last-box stationarity gives\n\\[\n(P_{N+1})_o\n=B(y)T_N^{(1)}(P_{N+1})_o\n+U(y)b_N^{\\mathrm{north}}(P_{N+1})_v.\n\\]\nAs \\(b=a(y)\\to\\infty\\), Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\n(P_{N+1})_v\\to P_N,\n\\qquad B(y)\\to1.\n\\]\nThus \\((P_{N+1})_o/U(y)\\) tends to the integrated north-source vector. Since\n\\[\nU(y)=\\frac{2d\\sin y}{b+C},\n\\]\nits numerator after multiplication by \\(p_N\\) is exactly\n\\[\n\\frac1{2d}[b^{N-1}]\\frac{(W_{N+1})_o}{\\sin y}.\n\\]\nProposition 2 bounds the Laurent exponents of this expression in each earlier variable by \\(2N\\).</p><p>Reflection from top to bottom reverses row order and replaces \\(u_i\\) by \\(3\\lambda-u_i\\); it preserves \\(p_N\\) and these bounds. Hence both \\(p_NH_N\\) and \\(p_NH_N^-\\) have the asserted bounds.</p><p>The pairing formula gives exponent bound \\(4N\\) for \\(p_N^2m\\). Separate reflection and \\(\\pi\\)-periodicity make it polynomial in each \\(a_i\\), of degree at most \\(2N\\). Indeed, a \\(\\pi\\)-periodic Laurent polynomial is Laurent polynomial in \\(z=e^{2iu_i}\\), and invariance under\n\\[\nz\\longmapsto e^{6i\\lambda}/z\n\\]\nmakes it polynomial in the corresponding scaled sum \\(a_i\\). Row exchange makes the polynomial symmetric.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 4.2 (Moment increments and bridge mass)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use homogeneous honeycomb strips with the port and critical-weight convention of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal A_N,\\mathcal B_N,m_N\\) be the arch mass, bridge mass, and first rightward arch-displacement moment defined there.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\lambda=\\pi/8\\), \\(c=\\cos(3\\lambda)\\), and use the rhombic weights of Proposition 1 at \\(u=\\lambda\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(N\\ge1\\),\n\\[\nc\\mathcal A_N+\\mathcal B_N=1.\n\\]\nThe bridge mass is nonincreasing, and\n\\[\nm_{N+1}-m_N\\asymp\\mathcal B_N\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(N\\).</p><p>More precisely, if one triangular row is added above the right half of \\(S_N\\), in columns \\(1,2,\\ldots\\), and \\(p\\) is the single side port at the left end of the new row, then\n\\[\nm_{N+1}-m_N\n=\\frac{\\sin\\lambda}{\\sin(3\\lambda)}\n\\sum_i Z(p,i),\n\\]\nwhere \\(i\\) runs over bottom ports of the stepped strip, and\n\\[\n\\rho\\mathcal B_N\n\\le\\sum_i Z(p,i)\n\\le\\frac{A(\\lambda)}{(1-B(\\lambda))^2}\\mathcal B_N.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply the boundary identity in Proposition 1 to parallelogram truncations of the strip. Left and right boundary terms tend to zero by transfer decay: after the source column, propagate in the one-source sector to the distant cut, where the exit is the only occupied boundary port. The remaining vacuum states are bounded. Other sums increase to their infinite-strip limits. Bottom exits have winding \\(\\pm\\pi\\), and top exits winding zero. Taking real parts gives\n\\[\nc\\mathcal A_N+\\mathcal B_N=1.\n\\]\nIncreasing strip height increases the arch sum by inclusion, so it decreases \\(\\mathcal B_N\\).</p><p>Add one triangular row above the right half of \\(S_N\\), and denote the stepped bottom arch kernel by \\(\\overline K(i,j)\\). From a bottom source \\(i\\), the imaginary part of the boundary identity is\n\\[\n\\sin(3\\lambda)\n\\left(\\sum_{j&lt;i}\\overline K(i,j)\n-\\sum_{j&gt;i}\\overline K(i,j)\\right)\n+\\sin\\lambda\\,Z(i,p)=0.\n\\]\nHorizontal top exits have winding zero, and closing along the leftward boundary gives winding \\(\\pi/3\\) at \\(p\\). Lateral truncation errors vanish as before, dominated by a strip of height \\(N+1\\).</p><p>Sum over \\(-L\\le i\\le L\\). Reversal cancels all internal arches. The remaining terms cross the two ends of this interval. After translating each end to the origin, the stepped domains converge locally to strips of heights \\(N\\) and \\(N+1\\), respectively. All crossing sums are dominated by the first moment in height \\(N+1\\), finite by transfer decay. This also controls paths leaving arbitrarily large lateral windows for each fixed port pair. Dominated convergence gives\n\\[\nm_{N+1}-m_N\n=\\frac{\\sin\\lambda}{\\sin(3\\lambda)}\\sum_iZ(p,i).\n\\]</p><p>Turning down immediately from \\(p\\) and following any bridge in the base strip gives\n\\[\n\\sum_iZ(p,i)\\ge\\rho\\mathcal B_N.\n\\]\nThe top-source bridge mass equals the bottom-source bridge mass by translation and reversal.</p><p>For the upper bound, order the path’s interface visits from left to right. In the new row, whose top is vacant, disjoint intervals pair \\(p\\) with the first visit and then pair subsequent visits consecutively. Base portions form a noncrossing pairing with one unpaired visit connected to the bottom; this defect cannot be enclosed. Since the union is one path, the first base visit must pair with its next neighbor: a farther pairing would leave a closed component inside. Removing those two visits and repeating shows that base visits also pair consecutively, with the final visit the defect. Thus the path encounters the interface in increasing horizontal order.</p><p>At \\(u=\\lambda\\), write \\(A=A(\\lambda)\\), \\(U=U(\\lambda)\\), \\(B=B(\\lambda)\\). The initial new-row segment has total weight at most \\(A/(1-B)\\). A one-sided base return arch has mass at most \\(1/(2c)\\), by the top-source boundary identity and equality of the two directions under translation and reversal. A one-sided return through the new row has total mass\n\\[\n\\frac{AU}{1-B}=2cB.\n\\]\nThe final base segment has mass \\(\\mathcal B_N\\). Ignoring avoidance between pieces only increases the sum, since the base weights are products over vertices. Therefore\n\\[\n\\sum_iZ(p,i)\n\\le\\frac{A}{1-B}\n\\sum_{r\\ge0}\\left(\\frac1{2c}\\,2cB\\right)^r\\mathcal B_N\n=\\frac{A}{(1-B)^2}\\mathcal B_N.\n\\]\nHere \\(B=\\rho^2&lt;1\\), so the constants are independent of \\(N\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 5.2 (Nonsingularity and integral representation)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\lambda=\\pi/8,\\quad\n  C=\\cos\\lambda,\\quad c=\\cos(3\\lambda),\\quad\n  d=\\cos(2\\lambda),\\quad \\eta=2d-1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g_X\\) be the monic quotient polynomial of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  h=b^2(1-b^2).\n  \\]\n  The homogeneous monic problem is to find a monic polynomial \\(g\\) of degree \\(N\\) such that\n  \\[\n  Q(b)=(b-C)^{2N+1}(b-c)^Ng(b)\n  =P(h)+bJ(h)+b^2\\mathcal E(h),\n  \\]\n  with\n  \\[\n  \\deg P,\\deg J\\le N,\\qquad \\deg\\mathcal E\\le N-1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\omega(x)=\\frac12\\sqrt{\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{2x}}-1},\n  \\qquad 0&lt;x&lt;1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;y_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;y_N&lt;1\\) have probability density proportional to\n  \\[\n  \\prod_{i=1}^N\\omega(y_i)\n  \\prod_{i&lt;j}\\frac{(y_j-y_i)^2}{y_i+y_j}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The homogeneous monic problem has a unique solution, and\n\\[\ng_X\\longrightarrow g\n\\qquad\\text{as all }a_i\\to C.\n\\]\nThe arch moment has the positive-integral representation\n\\[\nm_N=\\frac{\\eta}{\\pi}\n\\int_0^1\n\\left(1-\\mathbb E\\prod_{i=1}^N\\frac{y_i-x}{y_i+x}\\right)\n\\frac{\\omega(x)}x\\,dx.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 5.1–5.2 establish the unique homogeneous limit. It remains to identify the coefficient in the arch formula.</p><p>For the monic solution, the leading-coefficient calculation gives\n\\[\n[b^{4N}]Q=(-8)^N\\widetilde P(0).\n\\]\nComparing with\n\\[\nQ(b)=(b-C)^{2N+1}(b-c)^Ng(b)\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n[b^{N-1}]g(b)\n=\\frac{\\widetilde P(0)}{D_0}+(2N+1)C+Nc.\n\\]\nSubstitute into Proposition 3 and use \\(C=\\eta(2C+c)\\):\n\\[\nm_N=-\\eta\\left(C+\\frac{\\widetilde P(0)}{D_0}\\right).\n\\]</p><p>The branch function has representation\n\\[\nf(z)=C+\\frac1\\pi\\int_0^1\\frac{\\omega(x)}{x-z}\\,dx.\n\\]\nSubtract \\(D_0f\\) from the representation for \\(F\\), and let \\(z\\to0^-\\). Since\n\\[\n\\mathcal D(x)-D_0=O(x^{3/4}),\n\\]\nthe subtracted integrand is \\(O(x^{-1/2})\\) at zero, ensuring integrability and justifying the limit. On the left,\n\\[\nf(z)(\\widetilde J(z)-D_0)\\to0,\n\\qquad\nf(z)^2\\widetilde{\\mathcal E}(z)\\to0.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nm_N=\\frac{\\eta}{\\pi}\\int_0^1\n(1-G(x))\\frac{\\omega(x)}x\\,dx.\n\\]\nLemma 5.3 gives the required expectation formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1 (Missing harmonics)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For generic \\(X\\), put \\(b=\\cos w\\), and define\n  \\[\n  Q_X(b)=(b-C)g_X(b)\n  \\prod_{i=1}^N\n  (b-a_i)(\\cos(w-2\\lambda)-a_i)(\\cos(w+2\\lambda)-a_i),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\Phi_X(w)=\\sin w\\,Q_X(\\cos w).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The paired shifted factors have product\n  \\[\n  b^2-2da_ib+a_i^2-d^2,\n  \\]\n  so \\(Q_X\\) has degree \\(4N+1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every sine harmonic of \\(\\Phi_X\\) whose frequency is divisible by four has coefficient zero. Equivalently, with\n\\[\nh=b^2(1-b^2)=\\frac{1-\\cos4w}{8},\n\\]\nthere are polynomials satisfying\n\\[\nQ_X(b)=P(h)+bJ(h)+b^2\\mathcal E(h),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\deg P,\\deg J\\le N,\\qquad \\deg\\mathcal E\\le N-1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(i\\), and write \\(f_j=\\cos(v+j\\lambda)\\), with \\(f_0=a_i\\). The product defining \\(Q_X\\) gives\n\\[\n\\Phi_X(v\\pm2\\lambda)=0.\n\\]\nThe deletion quotient also gives\n\\[\n\\Phi_X(v+6\\lambda)+\\Phi_X(v-6\\lambda)=0.\n\\]</p><p>To verify sign and factors, cancel contributions of the other \\(a_j\\) in the ratio. On both sides those contributions involve exactly\n\\[\nf_6,f_{-6},f_4,f_{-4},-f_0.\n\\]\nThe remaining expressions are\n\\[\nR_\\pm=\n\\sin(v\\pm6\\lambda)(f_{\\pm6}-C)\n(f_0+f_{\\pm6}+\\rho)\n(f_{\\pm6}-f_0)(f_{\\pm4}-f_0).\n\\]\nProduct-to-sum and \\(\\rho=2cd\\) give\n\\[\nR_\\pm=\n\\cos(2v)(f_{\\pm6}-C)\n\\sin(v\\pm3\\lambda)(\\cos(v\\pm3\\lambda)+d).\n\\]\nAfter removing \\(\\cos(2v)\\), these are trigonometric polynomials of degree three. They have the same six zeros\n\\[\nv=\\pm3\\lambda,\\ \\pm5\\lambda,\\ \\pm7\\lambda\\pmod{2\\pi}\n\\]\nand opposite values at zero, hence are negatives of one another. This proves cancellation polynomially, including where canceled factors vanish.</p><p>Sum the four shifts of \\(\\Phi_X\\) by \\(\\pm2\\lambda,\\pm6\\lambda\\). Its multiplier on frequency \\(k\\) is\n\\[\n4\\cos(4k\\lambda)\\cos(2k\\lambda),\n\\]\nnonzero precisely when four divides \\(k\\). Its largest frequency is \\(4N+2\\). Dividing the shifted sum by \\(\\sin4w\\) gives a polynomial in \\(\\cos4w\\) of degree at most \\(N-1\\). The preceding identities give \\(N\\) distinct roots for generic \\(a_i\\), so this polynomial is zero.</p><p>Every polynomial in \\(b\\) has a unique expression\n\\[\nP_0(h)+bP_1(h)+b^2P_2(h)+b^3P_3(h),\n\\]\nobtained by repeatedly using \\(b^4=b^2-h\\). After multiplication by \\(\\sin w\\), the first three terms have no frequencies divisible by four. For the fourth,\n\\[\n\\sin w\\,b^3=\\frac14\\sin2w+\\frac18\\sin4w.\n\\]\nIts projection onto multiples of four is\n\\[\n\\frac18\\sin4w\\,P_3((1-\\cos4w)/8),\n\\]\nwhich vanishes only if \\(P_3=0\\). The degree bounds follow from \\(\\deg Q_X=4N+1\\).</p><p>At \\(a_i=C\\), the paired factors become \\((b-C)(b-c)\\), giving the homogeneous problem in Proposition 5.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Cauchy moment equations and nonsingularity in the proof of original Lemma 5.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Cauchy moment equations and nonsingularity in the proof of original Lemma 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the homogeneous polynomial problem and weight \\(\\omega\\) of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  z=(8h-1)^{-1},\n  \\qquad\n  b^2(1-b^2)=\\frac{1+1/z}{8}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\widetilde P(z)=z^NP((1+1/z)/8),\n  \\]\n  and similarly \\(\\widetilde J,\\widetilde{\\mathcal E}\\). These are polynomials, and \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal E}(0)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The branch tending to \\(C\\) at infinity and the sum of the two branches tending to \\(C,c\\) are\n  \\[\n  f(z)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\sqrt{(1-1/z)/2}}2},\n  \\qquad\n  l(z)=\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{(1+1/z)/2}}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  F(z)=\\widetilde P(z)+f(z)\\widetilde J(z)\n       +f(z)^2\\widetilde{\\mathcal E}(z),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D(z)=\\widetilde J(z)+l(z)\\widetilde{\\mathcal E}(z),\n  \\qquad\n  D_0=\\widetilde J(0).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a solution of the homogeneous polynomial problem,\n\\[\nF(z)=O(z^{-N-1}),\\qquad \\mathcal D(z)=O(1)\n\\qquad(z\\to\\infty),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nF(z)=\\frac1\\pi\\int_0^1\n\\frac{\\mathcal D(x)\\omega(x)}{x-z}\\,dx,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_0^1x^j\\mathcal D(x)\\omega(x)\\,dx=0,\n\\qquad0\\le j&lt;N,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal D(z)=D_0-\\frac{\\sqrt2}{\\pi}z\n\\int_0^1\n\\frac{\\widetilde{\\mathcal E}(-y)/y}{z+y}\\omega(y)\\,dy.\n\\]\nThese moment equations uniquely determine\n\\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal E}(-y)/y\\), of degree at most \\(N-1\\), from \\(D_0\\). They imply uniqueness and existence of the monic homogeneous problem and convergence \\(g_X\\to g\\).</p><p>For the monic solution,\n\\[\nD_0=(-8)^{-N},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal D(x)-D_0=O(x^{3/4})\\quad(x\\downarrow0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The prescribed zeros satisfy \\(h(C)=h(c)=1/8\\). The inverse branches differ from their limits by order \\(z^{-1}\\), since \\(h'(C)\\) and \\(h'(c)\\) are nonzero.</p><p>On the \\(C\\) branch, the order-\\((2N+1)\\) zero becomes \\(O(z^{-2N-1})\\); multiplication by \\(z^N\\) gives \\(F=O(z^{-N-1})\\). On the \\(c\\) branch, the order-\\(N\\) zero gives \\(O(1)\\) after multiplication. Subtracting the two branch expressions and dividing by their nonzero difference gives \\(\\mathcal D=O(1)\\).</p><p>The function \\(f\\) continues analytically off \\([0,1]\\), and \\(l\\) off \\([-1,0]\\). Their boundary jumps are\n\\[\nf_+(x)-f_-(x)=2i\\omega(x),\n\\]\n\\[\nl_+(-x)-l_-(-x)=-2\\sqrt2\\,i\\omega(x),\n\\qquad\nf_+(x)+f_-(x)=l(x).\n\\]\nAt the outer endpoints they are bounded. At zero,\n\\[\nF=O(|z|^{-1/4}),\n\\qquad \\mathcal D(z)\\to D_0,\n\\]\nthe latter using \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal E}(0)=0\\).</p><p>Cauchy’s formula, shrinking the small endpoint circles, gives the representation for \\(F\\). Its first \\(N\\) coefficients at infinity yield the vanishing moments. Subtracting the bounded-at-infinity representation for \\(\\mathcal D\\) at zero gives its displayed formula; its integrand is integrable because \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal E}(-y)/y\\) is polynomial.</p><p>Using \\(\\omega(y)=O(y^{-1/4})\\) and \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal E}(z)=O(z)\\), split at \\(y=x\\):\n\\[\n|\\mathcal D(x)-D_0|\n\\le C\\left(\\int_0^xy^{-1/4}\\,dy\n+x\\int_x^1y^{-5/4}\\,dy\\right)\n=O(x^{3/4}).\n\\]</p><p>The moment matrix, apart from nonzero scalar factors, is\n\\[\nM_{jk}=\\int_0^1\\int_0^1\n\\frac{x^{j+1}y^k}{x+y}\\omega(x)\\omega(y)\\,dx\\,dy,\n\\qquad 0\\le j,k&lt;N.\n\\]\nIts determinant is strictly positive. Expand and antisymmetrize successively in the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) variables. On ordered regions the integrand is the product of two positive Vandermondes, \\(\\prod_i x_i\\), positive weights, and\n\\[\n\\det\\left[\\frac1{x_i+y_j}\\right]\n=\\frac{\\prod_{i&lt;j}(x_j-x_i)(y_j-y_i)}\n       {\\prod_{i,j}(x_i+y_j)}&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor completeness, multiplying by the denominator leaves a numerator alternating in both families and of exactly the two Vandermondes’ degree. Its constant is one, recursively evaluating at \\(x_N=-y_N\\).</p><p>Consider the homogeneous linear problem for \\(g\\), with \\(\\deg g\\le N-1\\). Then \\([b^{4N+1}]Q=0\\), whereas\n\\[\n[b^{4N+1}]Q=(-8)^ND_0.\n\\]\nThus \\(D_0=0\\). Positivity forces \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal E}=0\\); the representation for \\(\\mathcal D\\) then gives \\(\\widetilde J=0\\), and the equation for \\(F\\) gives \\(\\widetilde P=0\\). Hence \\(g=0\\).</p><p>For a monic unknown\n\\[\ng(b)=b^N+\\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}\\gamma_rb^r,\n\\]\nthe excluded \\(b^3P_3(h)\\) part from Lemma 5.1 has \\(N\\) coefficients. Setting them to zero gives an \\(N\\times N\\) linear system for the \\(\\gamma_r\\). Injectivity proves invertibility and hence existence and uniqueness. Monicity also gives \\(D_0=(-8)^{-N}\\).</p><p>Apply the same coefficient conditions to \\(Q_X\\), replacing \\(g_X\\) by a monic unknown. The matrix and right side depend polynomially on the \\(a_i\\) through the zero factors and converge to those of the homogeneous system. They remain invertible for all \\(a_i\\) sufficiently close to \\(C\\). The generic \\(g_X\\) satisfies this system by Lemma 5.1, so it converges to \\(g\\). The moment itself is regular at the homogeneous point by Proposition 1.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Probabilistic solution of the moment equations in original Lemma 5.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Probabilistic solution of the moment equations in original Lemma 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((y_1,\\ldots,y_N)\\) have the law in Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  G(z)=\\mathcal D(z)/D_0\n  \\]\n  for the monic solution of Lemma 5.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nG(x)=\\mathbb E\\prod_{i=1}^N\\frac{y_i-x}{y_i+x}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The law is well defined: \\(\\omega(x)=O(x^{-1/4})\\) at zero, and every pair factor is at most \\(y_j\\le1\\). Its normalizing integral is finite and positive.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal W_n\\) denote the density product with \\(n\\) coordinates, regarded on \\((0,1)^n\\) without ordering, and use its normalized symmetric law for \\(n=N\\).</p><p>Partial fractions give\n\\[\n\\prod_{i=1}^N\\frac{y_i-x}{y_i+x}\n=1-x\\sum_{i=1}^N\\frac{\\alpha_i}{x+y_i},\n\\qquad\n\\alpha_i=2\\prod_{j\\ne i}\\frac{y_j+y_i}{y_j-y_i}.\n\\]\nAfter multiplication by the density, the factors in \\(\\alpha_i\\) cancel one Vandermonde and all corresponding denominator factors. Each canceled term is integrable: remaining polynomial factors and pair kernels are bounded on \\((0,1)^N\\), as is \\(x/(x+y_i)\\), and the one-variable weights are integrable. Integrating the other variables gives\n\\[\n1-x\\int_0^1\\frac{P(y)\\omega(y)}{x+y}\\,dy,\n\\qquad \\deg P\\le N-1.\n\\]</p><p>Its moments against \\(x^k\\omega(x)\\) vanish for \\(k&lt;N\\). To see this, adjoin \\(x\\) as an \\((N+1)\\)st variable and use\n\\[\nx^k\\omega(x)\\mathcal W_N(y)\n\\prod_{i=1}^N\\frac{y_i-x}{y_i+x}\n=\n\\frac{x^k\\mathcal W_{N+1}(x,y)}\n     {\\prod_{i=1}^N(y_i-x)}.\n\\]\nThe left side is absolutely integrable because the product has modulus at most one. Symmetrizing the right side in all \\(N+1\\) variables gives zero by\n\\[\n\\sum_{r=0}^N\n\\frac{z_r^k}{\\prod_{j\\ne r}(z_j-z_r)}=0,\n\\qquad0\\le k&lt;N.\n\\]\nThus the expectation has the same polynomial Cauchy representation and vanishing moments as \\(G\\). The positive moment-matrix uniqueness from Lemma 5.2 identifies them.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Lemma 6.1 (Association and monotone comparison)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\) be an interval, \\(n\\ge1\\), and let \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_n\\) be positive smooth functions on \\(I\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  K(x,y)=\\frac{(y-x)^2}{x+y}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  \\prod_{i=1}^nw_i(x_i)\\prod_{i&lt;j}K(x_i,x_j),\n  \\qquad x_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;x_n,\\quad x_i\\in I,\n  \\]\n  has finite positive normalizing integral.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The resulting probability law is positively associated: increasing integrable functions have nonnegative covariance whenever that covariance is defined. The assertion also holds after truncation to a shorter common interval.</p><p>Consequently, a coordinatewise increasing likelihood ratio shifts the coordinates upwards in stochastic order, and a decreasing likelihood ratio shifts them downwards.</p>", "proof": "<p>The pair contribution satisfies\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial x\\partial y}\\log K(x,y)\n=\\frac2{(y-x)^2}+\\frac1{(x+y)^2}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Prove association by induction on the number of coordinates, initially for bounded increasing functions. Conditional on the largest coordinate \\(t\\), the remaining coordinates have a law of the same form on a shorter interval, with modified one-variable factors, and are associated by induction.</p><p>If \\(s&lt;t\\), the ratio of the conditional density at \\(s\\) to that at \\(t\\), on the former support, is coordinatewise decreasing by the mixed-derivative inequality. Extended by zero when a coordinate exceeds \\(s\\), it remains decreasing on the latter support. Association at \\(t\\) shows that a decreasing likelihood tilt decreases each increasing expectation. If the ratio is unbounded, truncate it and pass to the limit by monotone convergence. Thus the conditional expectation of any increasing function of all coordinates increases with \\(t\\).</p><p>The law of total covariance has two nonnegative terms: conditional covariance by induction, and covariance of the two conditional expectations by\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Cov}(F(T),G(T))\n=\\frac12\\mathbb E[(F(T)-F(T'))(G(T)-G(T'))]\\ge0,\n\\]\nwhere \\(T'\\) is an independent copy. Truncation and integrable approximation extend the result to the stated class.</p><p>Finally, for increasing likelihood ratio \\(L\\) and increasing \\(F\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathbb E[FL]}{\\mathbb EL}\\ge\\mathbb EF;\n\\]\nthe inequality reverses for decreasing \\(L\\). This proves stochastic comparison.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 6.2 (Laguerre normalization and coordinate bounds)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For \\(a&gt;0\\), let the generalized Bures–Laguerre law have ordered density\n  \\[\n  \\frac1{Z_n(a)}\n  \\prod_{i=1}^nr_i^{a-1}e^{-r_i}\n  \\prod_{i&lt;j}\\frac{(r_j-r_i)^2}{r_i+r_j},\n  \\qquad 0&lt;r_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;r_n&lt;\\infty.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Its expectations and probabilities are denoted \\(\\mathbb E_{n,a}\\) and \\(\\mathbb P_{n,a}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(Z_0(a)=1\\) and\n  \\[\n  D_k=ak+\\frac{k(k-1)}2.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(n\\ge1\\),\n\\[\nZ_n(a)=\n\\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}\\Gamma(a+i)\n\\prod_{0\\le i&lt;j&lt;n}\\frac{j-i}{2a+i+j}.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(a&gt;0\\), there are constants \\(K_a,C_a\\), depending only on \\(a\\), such that\n\\(\\mathbb P_{n,a}(r_n\\le K_an)\\) is bounded below by a positive constant independent of \\(n\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_{n,a}(r_k&lt;h)\n\\le\\min\\left\\{1,\n\\left(\\frac{C_ahn^2}{k^3}\\right)^{D_k}\\right\\},\n\\quad h&gt;0,\\quad1\\le k\\le n.\n\\]\nIn particular, with absolute implicit constants,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{n,1/2}\\left(\\sum_i r_i^{-1}\\right)^{1/4}\n\\lesssim n^{1/2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{n,2}\\sum_i r_i^{-1}\\lesssim n^2,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_{n,1}r_1^{-1/4}\\gtrsim n^{1/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.1 gives the normalizing constants. Lemma 7.2 gives maximum control, small-coordinate tails, and the two upper inverse-moment estimates. Lemma 7.3 gives the minimum-coordinate lower estimate. These are all assertions of the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Normalization portion of original Lemma 6.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Normalization portion of original Lemma 6.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the ordered Bures–Laguerre law with \\(a&gt;0\\) and \\(n\\ge1\\) from Proposition 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nZ_n(a)=\n\\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}\\Gamma(a+i)\n\\prod_{0\\le i&lt;j&lt;n}\\frac{j-i}{2a+i+j}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write the pair product as a Vandermonde determinant times Schur’s Pfaffian:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pf}\\left[\\frac{x_j-x_i}{x_j+x_i}\\right]\n=\\prod_{i&lt;j}\\frac{x_j-x_i}{x_j+x_i}.\n\\]\nFor even size, clearing denominators leaves an alternating polynomial of Vandermonde degree. Pairing two opposite arguments checks its constant recursively as one. For odd size, add an argument tending to infinity, producing an augmenting column of ones.</p><p>Expand the determinant and Pfaffian and integrate over the unordered region, divided by \\(n!\\). De Bruijn’s determinant–Pfaffian integration formula gives the Pfaffian of double moments, with single moments as augmenting entries for odd size. Absolute integrability follows from\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{s-r}{s+r}\\right|\\le1\n\\]\nand finite gamma moments.</p><p>The double moment indexed by \\(i,j\\) is\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty\\int_0^\\infty\nr^{a+i-1}s^{a+j-1}e^{-r-s}\\frac{s-r}{s+r}\\,dr\\,ds\n=\n\\Gamma(a+i)\\Gamma(a+j)\\frac{j-i}{2a+i+j}.\n\\]\nUse \\(r+s\\) and \\(r/(r+s)\\); the latter beta integral has mean\n\\((a+i)/(2a+i+j)\\).\nFactor out the gamma terms and apply Schur’s identity again at arguments \\(a+i\\). This yields the normalizer.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Coordinate tails and upper inverse-moment bounds from original Lemma 6.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Coordinate tails and upper inverse-moment bounds from original Lemma 6.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 7 with fixed \\(a&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(D_k=ak+k(k-1)/2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are constants \\(K_a,C_a\\) as in Proposition 7, and, for \\(0&lt;p&lt;D_k\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{n,a}r_k^{-p}\n\\le\\frac{D_k}{D_k-p}\n\\left(\\frac{C_an^2}{k^3}\\right)^p.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{n,1/2}\\left(\\sum_i r_i^{-1}\\right)^{1/4}\n\\lesssim n^{1/2},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_{n,2}\\sum_i r_i^{-1}\\lesssim n^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the maximum, condition on \\(r_n=s\\). Every factor involving a smaller coordinate is at most \\(s\\), since \\(K(r,s)\\le s\\) for \\(r&lt;s\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_{n,a}(r_n&gt;Kn)\n\\le\\frac{Z_{n-1}(a)}{Z_n(a)}\n\\int_{Kn}^\\infty s^{a+n-2}e^{-s}\\,ds.\n\\]\nLemma 7.1 gives\n\\[\n\\frac{Z_{n-1}(a)}{Z_n(a)}\n=\\frac1{\\Gamma(a+n-1)}\n\\prod_{j=1}^{n-1}\\frac{2a+2n-2-j}{j}\n\\le\\frac{e^a(2e)^{n-1}}{\\Gamma(a+n-1)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(n\\ge2\\), replace each numerator by \\(2(a+n-1)\\), use\n\\((n-1)!\\ge((n-1)/e)^{n-1}\\), and\n\\((1+a/(n-1))^{n-1}\\le e^a\\). The case \\(n=1\\) is immediate.</p><p>A gamma exponential-moment bound gives\n\\[\n\\frac1{\\Gamma(a+n-1)}\n\\int_{Kn}^\\infty s^{a+n-2}e^{-s}\\,ds\n\\le e^{-Kn/2}2^{a+n-1}.\n\\]\nChoosing \\(K\\) large makes the maximum tail uniformly small, in fact exponentially small in \\(n\\).</p><p>For the small-coordinate estimate, integrate the first \\(k\\) coordinates below \\(h\\). Their mutual pair factors are at most \\(h\\); each factor between a small coordinate and a remaining coordinate is at most the latter. Dropping order constraints between the groups gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_{n,a}(r_k&lt;h)\n\\le \\frac{h^{D_k}}{a^kk!}\n\\frac{Z_{n-k}(a+k)}{Z_n(a)}.\n\\]\nFrom the normalizer,\n\\[\n\\frac{Z_{n-k}(a+k)}{Z_n(a)}\n=\n\\prod_{i=0}^{k-1}\\Gamma(a+i)^{-1}\n\\prod_{\\substack{0\\le i&lt;j&lt;n\\\\i&lt;k}}\n\\left(1+\\frac{2a+2i}{j-i}\\right).\n\\]\nFor gaps \\(j-i\\le k\\), use \\(2a+2i\\le C_ak\\). For each \\(i&lt;k\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^k\\log(1+C_ak/j)\n\\le k\\log(1+C_a)+k\\log k-\\log(k!)\n=O_a(k).\n\\]\nShort gaps contribute \\(O_a(k^2)=O_a(D_k)\\). Larger gaps contribute at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{i&lt;k}(2a+2i)\n\\sum_{j=k+1}^{n-1-i}\\frac1j\n\\le2D_k\\log(n/k).\n\\]\nFor \\(k=n\\), these latter sums are empty.</p><p>Furthermore,\n\\[\n\\prod_{i=0}^{k-1}\\Gamma(a+i)\n\\ge k^{D_k}e^{-O_a(D_k)}.\n\\]\nIndeed, for \\(q=a+i\\), integration on \\([q,q+1]\\) gives\n\\[\n\\log\\Gamma(q)\\ge(q-1)\\log q-q-O_a(1).\n\\]\nSum, separate \\(\\log k\\), and use\n\\[\n\\sum_{i&lt;k}(a+i)\\log((a+i)/k)\\ge-k^2/e.\n\\]\nThe missing \\(k\\log k\\) is absorbed in \\(O_a(D_k)\\), adjusting the constant for bounded \\(k\\).</p><p>Combining these estimates, absorbing \\(a^{-k}/k!\\) into \\(C_a^{D_k}\\), proves\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_{n,a}(r_k&lt;h)\n\\le\\min\\left\\{1,\n\\left(\\frac{C_ahn^2}{k^3}\\right)^{D_k}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nIntegrating the tail yields the stated inverse-moment bound.</p><p>At \\(a=1/2\\), use \\((k,p)=(1,1/4)\\), then \\(p=1,k\\ge2\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbb Er_1^{-1/4}\\lesssim n^{1/2},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{k\\ge2}r_k^{-1}\\lesssim n^2.\n\\]\nSubadditivity of the fourth root and Jensen’s inequality give the first asserted upper bound. At \\(a=2\\), use \\(p=1\\) for every \\(k\\) and sum to obtain the second.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Minimum-coordinate lower bound from original Lemma 6.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Minimum-coordinate lower bound from original Lemma 6.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the ordered Bures–Laguerre law with \\(a=1\\) and \\(n\\ge1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With an absolute constant,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{n,1}r_1^{-1/4}\\gtrsim n^{1/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The density of the smallest coordinate at \\(s&gt;0\\) is\n\\[\ne^{-s}\\frac{Z_{n-1}(2)}{Z_n(1)}\n\\mathbb E_{n-1,2}\\left[\n\\mathbf1_{\\{r_i&gt;s\\ \\forall i\\}}\n\\prod_i\\frac{(1-s/r_i)^2}{1+s/r_i}\n\\right].\n\\]\nLemma 7.1 gives\n\\[\n\\frac{Z_{n-1}(2)}{Z_n(1)}=\\frac{n(n+1)}2.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nq_s(r)=\\mathbf1_{\\{r&gt;s\\}}\n\\frac{(1-s/r)^2}{1+s/r}.\n\\]\nFor all \\(r&gt;0\\), including \\(r\\le s\\),\n\\[\n0\\le q_s(r)\\le1,\\qquad 1-q_s(r)\\le3s/r.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\prod_iq_s(r_i)\\ge1-3s\\sum_i r_i^{-1}.\n\\]\nBy Lemma 7.2, the expectation is at least \\(1/2\\) for\n\\(0&lt;s&lt;\\varepsilon n^{-2}\\), for a sufficiently small fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\). The minimum density is then bounded below by a positive multiple of \\(n^2\\). An interval with endpoints fixed positive multiples of \\(n^{-2}\\) has probability bounded below, and on it\n\\[\nr_1^{-1/4}\\gtrsim n^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThe case \\(n=1\\) is also covered directly.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Homogeneous arch-moment growth.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Homogeneous arch-moment growth.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m_N\\) be the homogeneous honeycomb arch moment.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;y_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;y_N&lt;1\\) have density\n  \\[\n  \\frac1{\\mathcal Z_N}\n  \\prod_i\\omega(y_i)\\prod_{i&lt;j}\\frac{(y_j-y_i)^2}{y_i+y_j},\n  \\qquad\n  \\omega(y)=\\frac12\\sqrt{\\sqrt{\\frac{1+y}{2y}}-1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the Bures–Laguerre reference laws and notation of Proposition 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly for \\(N\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb Ey_1^{-1/4}\\lesssim m_N\n\\lesssim\\mathbb E\\left(\\sum_i y_i^{-1}\\right)^{1/4},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nm_N\\asymp N^{3/4}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed coordinates, put\n\\[\nR(x)=\\prod_i\\frac{y_i-x}{y_i+x}.\n\\]\nEach factor lies in \\([-1,1]\\), so \\(1-R(x)\\ge0\\). Telescoping gives\n\\[\n1-R(x)\\le2\\min\\left\\{1,x\\sum_i y_i^{-1}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(y_1/3&lt;x&lt;y_1/2\\), all factors are nonnegative and the first is at most \\(1/2\\); hence \\(1-R(x)\\ge1/2\\). Also,\n\\[\n\\omega(x)\\lesssim x^{-1/4}\\quad(0&lt;x&lt;1),\n\\qquad\n\\omega(x)\\asymp x^{-1/4}\\quad(0&lt;x&lt;1/2).\n\\]\nIn the integral of Proposition 5, integrate the lower bound on that interval, and split the upper integral at\n\\[\nx=\\left(\\sum_i y_i^{-1}\\right)^{-1}.\n\\]\nThis proves\n\\[\n\\mathbb Ey_1^{-1/4}\\lesssim m_N\n\\lesssim\\mathbb E\\left(\\sum_i y_i^{-1}\\right)^{1/4}.\n\\]</p><p>For the upper estimate, condition the target law on \\(y_N&lt;1/2\\). This decreasing event shifts coordinates downwards by Proposition 6, increasing the inverse statistic. On the shorter interval compare with\n\\[\ny_i=\\frac{r_i}{bN}\n\\]\nunder the \\(a=1/2\\) reference law, conditioned on \\(r_N&lt;bN/2\\). Up to a constant, the target-to-reference likelihood ratio is a product of\n\\[\n\\omega(y)y^{1/2}e^{bNy}.\n\\]\nDirect differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dy}\\log(\\omega(y)y^{1/2})\n=\n\\frac{1-2y-\\sqrt{2y/(1+y)}}{4y(1-y)}\n\\ge-4,\n\\qquad0&lt;y\\le1/2.\n\\]\nThe numerator is positive for \\(y\\le1/8\\); on the remaining interval it is at least \\(-1\\), and the denominator is at least \\(1/4\\). Thus the likelihood factors are increasing for all \\(N\\ge1\\) once \\(b\\ge4\\).</p><p>Association shows that the conditioned reference law has the larger inverse statistic. Also choose \\(b/2\\ge K_{1/2}\\), so its conditioning probability is bounded below uniformly in \\(N\\), by Proposition 7. Therefore\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\left(\\sum_i y_i^{-1}\\right)^{1/4}\n\\lesssim\n(bN)^{1/4}\n\\mathbb E_{N,1/2}\\left(\\sum_i r_i^{-1}\\right)^{1/4}\n\\lesssim N^{3/4}.\n\\]</p><p>For the lower estimate, substitute\n\\[\ny_i=\\frac{2z_i}{1+z_i},\n\\qquad0&lt;z_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;z_N&lt;1.\n\\]\nThe pair kernel transforms as\n\\[\nK\\!\\left(\\frac{2z_i}{1+z_i},\\frac{2z_j}{1+z_j}\\right)\n=\n\\frac{2K(z_i,z_j)}\n{(1+z_i)(1+z_j)\n (1+2z_iz_j/(z_i+z_j))}.\n\\]\nTogether with the Jacobian, this gives one-variable factors\n\\[\n\\omega(2z/(1+z))(1+z)^{-(N+1)}\n\\]\nand additional pair factors\n\\[\n(1+2z_iz_j/(z_i+z_j))^{-1}.\n\\]</p><p>Compare with \\(z_i=r_i/(bN)\\) under the \\(a=1\\) reference law, with fixed \\(0&lt;b&lt;1/2\\), conditioned on \\(r_N&lt;bN\\). The likelihood ratio has one-variable factors\n\\[\n\\omega\\!\\left(\\frac{2z}{1+z}\\right)\n(1+z)^{-(N+1)}e^{bNz}\n\\]\nand the displayed pair factors. Each is decreasing in every coordinate: \\(\\omega\\) is decreasing, and\n\\[\n-\\frac{N+1}{1+z}+bN&lt;0,\\qquad0&lt;z&lt;1.\n\\]\nProposition 6 puts the target \\(z\\) coordinates below those of the conditioned reference, which are themselves below the unconditioned reference coordinates. Since \\(y_1\\le2z_1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb Ey_1^{-1/4}\n\\ge2^{-1/4}(bN)^{1/4}\n\\mathbb E_{N,1}r_1^{-1/4}\n\\gtrsim N^{3/4}\n\\]\nby Proposition 7.</p><p>Combining both comparisons with the integral bounds proves \\(m_N\\asymp N^{3/4}\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 5, "cite"], [1, 7, "cite"], [1, 8, "cite"], [1, 9, "cite"], [1, 11, "cite"], [1, 14, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [2, 4, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 8, "cite"], [5, 10, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 0, "cite"], [8, 12, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [12, 21, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 12, "cite"], [16, 21, "cite"], [17, 21, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 20, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 17, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"]]});
